      en: "Lei Yue Mun Fort at the western tip of Hong Kong Island was built in the 1880s by the British to defend the eastern approaches to the harbour. It was part of Hong Kong's coastal defence, equipped with heavy guns. During the Battle of Hong Kong in World War II, the fort saw heavy fighting and eventually fell to the Japanese. After the war, it was abandoned and fell into disrepair. In the 1990s, it was restored and turned into a public park. Today, visitors can explore the gun emplacements, bunkers, and quarters. The fort offers insight into Hong Kong's military history and the strategic importance of the Lei Yue Mun strait.",
